class Solution {
    public int maxSubArray(int[] nums) {
        // Initialize with the first element
        int currentSum = nums[0];
        int maxSum = nums[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
            // Choice: Start fresh at nums[i] or add nums[i] to current sum
            currentSum = Math.max(nums[i], currentSum + nums[i]);
            
            // Update the global maximum if current sum is better
            maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, currentSum);
        }

        return maxSum;
    }
}



In Java, the easiest way to write a DFS is using recursion. It uses the "Call Stack" to keep track of where you are
Binary Tree
class Solution {
    public void dfs(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return;

        // 1. Process current node
        System.out.println(root.val);

        // 2. Go deep into left child
        dfs(root.left);

        // 3. Go deep into right child
        dfs(root.right);
    }
}

Graph
class Solution {
    public void dfs(int node, List<Integer>[] adj, boolean[] visited) {
        // Mark current node as visited
        visited[node] = true;
        System.out.println("Visited: " + node);

        // Visit all neighbors that haven't been seen yet
        for (int neighbor : adj[node]) {
            if (!visited[neighbor]) {
                dfs(neighbor, adj, visited);
            }
        }
    }
}


